<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudolph of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/936">936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Raoul of France, Rudolph of West Francia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rudolph was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 921 and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> from 923 until his death in 936, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-the-justiciar">Richard the Justiciar</a>. Elected by the nobles in place of the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> and struggled to win recognition in the south. He died without children, and the Carolingian Louis IV was recalled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudolph-of-france.jpg" alt="Rudolph of France" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grandes Chroniques de France · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Rudolph_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rudolph was born around 890, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-the-justiciar">Richard the Justiciar</a> and Adelaide of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> documents his name is usually Rodulfus, and he always used that form himself, as on his seal, although he is often called Raoul in modern French and Ralph in English. He inherited the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from his father in 921 and married Emma, daughter of King Robert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>Robert was killed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> fighting the deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, and Rudolph was elected to succeed him. He was crowned by Walter, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>, at Saint-Médard de Soissons on 13 July 923 and passed the duchy to his younger brother, Hugh the Black.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rudolph first led an army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, who had made a pact with Robert and tried to annex Lotharingia. Peace was made, but in 925 Henry took Lotharingia for good, and Rudolph could not resist. In 924 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> from the Loire Valley, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and moved to Melun. Rudolph raised troops with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, and then with Herbert and Arnulf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> near Fauquembergues, where the king was wounded.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> invaded the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a> in 930 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in 935, and Rudolph's large army made them retreat without a battle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> was temporarily safe from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and Magyars at his death.</p><p>Herbert II of Vermandois held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> prisoner and used him to win the archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a> for his son Hugh and the county of Laon for his son Odo. Charles died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a>, and Rudolph was not fully recognised in the south until Charles died in captivity on 7 October 929. He received the homage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-longsword">William Longsword</a> and in 933 granted him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>. In 931 he replaced Hugh at Rheims with Artald, and joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a> he burned Herbert's fortresses and cornered him in Château-Thierry.</p><p>In the south he also gave the title of prince of Gothia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-pons">Raymond Pons</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and campaigned against the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> strongholds. The two men made peace in 935. Rudolph fell ill and died on 14 or 15 January 936, leaving no sons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rudolph of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Rudolph</b> became King of West Francia in 923, and the duchy later passed through several hands until Henry the Great, brother of Hugh Capet, ruled it from 965 to 1002.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rollo, baptised with the name Robert, extended his territory, and in 924 King <b>Rudolph of West Francia</b> granted the Normans further lands, including the Bessin around Bayeux.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.8,3.57&amp;year=926" aria-label="Auxerre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4633 489 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Rudolph of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5083 759h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5083 759h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.8,3.57&amp;year=926">What was here in 926 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
